          "text": "# Sham, the Arrow\u2019s Quiet Golden Point\n\n*In the small constellation Sagitta, a modest naked-eye star offers a lesson in how astronomers measure distance\u2014and how much a steady point of light can hold.*\n\nOn a clear summer evening, find Altair, the bright star in Aquila. Look just north of it for Sagitta, a faint little arrow among the richer star fields of the Milky Way. Its star Sham\u2014catalogued as Alpha Sagittae, or HIP 96757\u2014is not a blaze but a patient glimmer. At about magnitude 4.4, it is visible to the unaided eye under a reasonably dark sky, though nearby city lights may wash it out.\n\nThe arrow\u2019s outline is small enough to miss at first. Give your eyes a few minutes to adjust, then scan slowly around Altair. Binoculars can help reveal the surrounding stars and settle your view on the pattern; use them to explore, not to sweep across the sky while walking. Sagitta is the constellation to look for, not a dramatically bright individual star announcing itself.\n\nSham sits nearly 131 parsecs away\u2014about 427 light-years\u2014according to its measured parallax. Parallax is the apparent shift of a nearby star against more distant background stars as Earth travels around the Sun. The shift is tiny: SIMBAD lists Sham\u2019s parallax as 7.6353 milliarcseconds. Convert that angle into distance, and a familiar point of light becomes a far-off sun whose photons began their journey centuries ago.\n\nIts catalogued spectral type is G1II. The G tells us it belongs to a broad class of stars with temperatures in the same general neighborhood as the Sun\u2019s; the II indicates a bright giant, a star in a more luminous stage than an ordinary main-sequence star like our Sun. That label is a classification, not a photograph of the star\u2019s surface. It helps astronomers place Sham among other stars and compare their physical properties.\n\nThe name and the catalogue designation offer different ways to meet the same object. \u201cSham\u201d gives the star a handle for the observing night; \u201cAlpha Sagittae\u201d places it in a formal system tied to its constellation. The SIMBAD astronomical database identifies HIP 96757 as Alpha Sagittae and records its coordinates, parallax, spectral type and measured brightness. Its listed visual magnitude, 4.38, explains why Sham rewards attention rather than demanding it.\n\nFor a simple observing note, record the date, your location, and whether you could see the arrow without optical aid. Try again on a darker night, or compare the view through binoculars. The star will not visibly change from one session to the next, but your conditions\u2014and your ability to pick out the pattern\u2014may. That is part of the pleasure: the sky is not only a display but a place to practice noticing.\n\nSham\u2019s light reaches us from a distance that is hard to picture, yet its quiet point is accessible from a backyard or a patch of open sky. Find the small arrow, let your eyes settle, and give the faint star time to emerge. Then look up knowing that its measured distance comes not from guesswork, but from the subtle geometry of Earth\u2019s orbit.\n\n**Sources:** [SIMBAD entry for HIP 96757](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2096757)."
